<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Unraid Community Applications template for cutoffarr.

  This file is deliberately kept in field-for-field agreement with
  docker-compose.example.yml at the repo root, which is the canonical,
  human-verified deployment shape (port 9898, the /config volume mounted
  read-only, the Unraid uid:gid pair 99:100, the unless-stopped restart
  policy, the 90s stop_grace_period, the icon URL, and both Unraid labels'
  worth of caveats). template_test.go pins the two files against each other,
  the same way container_test.go pins the Dockerfile against the Go code it
  packages — this file is documentation nothing in the Go build reads, and
  is exactly the kind of thing that rots silently otherwise.

  Repository intentionally points at the GHCR image release.yml publishes on
  a tag push (ghcr.io/foley-ops/cutoffarr) — nothing about committing this
  template pushes an image or submits it to Community Applications; that is
  a deliberately separate, human-triggered step.
-->
<Container version="2">
  <Name>cutoffarr</Name>
  <Repository>ghcr.io/foley-ops/cutoffarr</Repository>
  <Registry>https://github.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr/pkgs/container/cutoffarr</Registry>
  <Branch/>
  <NetworkType>custom</NetworkType>
  <!--
    "media-net" is docker-compose.example.yml's own placeholder for "whatever
    external network your *arr stack is actually on" — compose.yml is explicit
    that a network Compose created for another stack is named
    "<project>_default", not the bare name from that stack's file, and the
    same caveat applies here. Change this to your real *arr network's name
    (Docker Settings, or `docker network ls`) before starting the container;
    left as "media-net", it will only work if a network with that exact name
    already exists.
  -->
  <Network>media-net</Network>
  <MyIP/>
  <!--
    Unraid's Console button runs whatever this names inside the container.
    The final image is `gcr.io/distroless/static:nonroot` — no shell, no
    package manager, nothing executable but the one binary — so the Console
    button WILL fail no matter what this says. This is set to "sh" only
    because Unraid requires some value here and because it matches
    docker-compose.example.yml's own net.unraid.docker.shell label, which
    carries the identical caveat. Diagnose with the Log button
    (`docker logs cutoffarr`) instead — everything this program has to say is
    on stdout.
  -->
  <Shell>sh</Shell>
  <Privileged>false</Privileged>
  <Support>https://github.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr/issues</Support>
  <Project>https://github.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr</Project>
  <Overview>cutoffarr unmonitors Radarr movies and Sonarr seasons once they have genuinely finished upgrading (quality cutoff AND custom-format cutoff score both reached), so your indexers stop being asked to search for something no release will ever provide. It never deletes, moves, or renames a file, and the only field it ever writes is `monitored` -- never quality profiles, tags, or any other setting. dry_run defaults to true: a fresh deployment writes nothing until you edit config.yml and explicitly turn it off. See the README (https://github.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr#readme) for the full config reference, the safety/dry-run guarantees, and how to point each *arr's Settings -> Connect -> Webhook at this container. Mount your config.yml (copied from config.example.yml) at the Config path below; API keys referenced in it as ${VAR} are read from the container's own environment, which you can supply with additional "Variable" entries on this template (Add another Path, Port, Variable...).</Overview>
  <Category>Tools:</Category>
  <!--
    net.unraid.docker.webui / the <WebUI> element is DELIBERATELY OMITTED,
    not forgotten: cutoffarr has no web UI at all. The one HTTP endpoint the
    container exposes is POST /webhook/{instance-name}, which every *arr's
    own Connect -> Webhook setting talks to directly — a human clicking
    Unraid's "WebUI" button would only ever see a bare 405 Method Not
    Allowed, since the endpoint accepts POST and nothing else. Leaving the
    element out entirely is what keeps that button itself from appearing.
  -->
  <TemplateURL>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr/main/templates/cutoffarr.xml</TemplateURL>
  <Icon>https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Foley-ops/cutoffarr/main/icon.png</Icon>
  <!--
    The ExtraParams below sets three things docker-compose.example.yml also
    sets, since Unraid's own Config fields have no equivalent for any of
    them: the user 99:100 override, matching the compose file's
    `user: "99:100"` (the image's own default is distroless's nonroot,
    65532:65532; Unraid's usual nobody:users pair is what makes the
    read-only /config mount below actually readable); restart
    unless-stopped, matching the compose file's restart policy; and
    stop-timeout 90, matching its stop_grace_period: 90s. See that file's
    own long comment for why 90s specifically (a Sonarr season write is an
    atomic pair of API calls deliberately detached from shutdown, and
    Docker's DEFAULT stop timeout of 10s is short enough to SIGKILL the
    process between the two, leaving one episode-level write applied and the
    season-level write never sent). Do not shorten stop-timeout without
    reading that comment first.
  -->
  <ExtraParams>--user 99:100 --restart unless-stopped --stop-timeout 90</ExtraParams>
  <PostArgs/>
  <CPUset/>
  <DateInstalled/>
  <DonateText/>
  <DonateLink/>
  <Requires/>
  <Config Name="Config" Target="/config" Default="/mnt/user/appdata/cutoffarr" Mode="ro" Description="Directory holding config.yml (copied from this repo's config.example.yml). Mounted read-only: cutoffarr never writes to disk." Type="Path" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false">/mnt/user/appdata/cutoffarr</Config>
  <Config Name="Webhook Port" Target="9898" Default="9898" Mode="tcp" Description="POST /webhook/{instance-name} -- point each *arr's Settings -> Connect -> Webhook at http://cutoffarr:9898/webhook/&lt;instance-name&gt; on the shared network above; publishing this port is only needed to reach the endpoint from outside Docker." Type="Port" Display="always" Required="true" Mask="false">9898</Config>
</Container>
